0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","2.7",{"topic":{"id":"2.7","title":"Evidence Retrieval Techniques","minutes":12,"summary":"Recover information through controlled exports, backups, filesystem analysis, and carving—while recording the limits of each method.","sections":[{"title":"Logical, filesystem, and physical access","body":["A logical export uses an application or operating-system interface and may include only records visible to that interface. A filesystem acquisition extracts files and available metadata through the device’s storage abstraction. A physical acquisition seeks a lower-level representation where supported and lawful. These methods differ in completeness and in their ability to access deleted or protected areas.","Choose the least destructive method that can answer the question. A supported mail export preserving headers may be more useful than an indiscriminate disk image for one conversation. A mobile “full extraction” label is product-specific and does not guarantee every app, encrypted record, or deleted message. Record the device model, OS, tool, method, and known limitations."]},{"title":"Deleted data and file carving","body":["Deleting a file often changes filesystem references before the underlying content is overwritten. Recovery may be possible from unallocated space, journal records, shadows, backups, or cloud versions. On SSDs, TRIM and garbage collection can make older recovery assumptions unreliable. Success depends on the device, filesystem, elapsed activity, encryption, and the specific deletion process.","File carving identifies files using headers, footers, or internal structures rather than filesystem entries. Fragmentation can defeat simple contiguous carving, and a recovered stream may lack its original filename, path, or timestamps. Do not silently assign recovered data to a user based only on its presence in unallocated space. Preserve the source location and recovery method."]},{"title":"Recovery is a process, not a promise","body":["Use verified images or controlled exports, calculate hashes, and record tool settings. Work on copies so that recovery attempts do not alter the preserved source. Compare recovered records with independent sources such as mail attachments, backups, or provider logs. A match can strengthen context but does not erase uncertainty about who created the original.","Document unsuccessful attempts as well as successes. “No recoverable copy was found using this method” is different from “the file never existed”. Explain whether encryption, unsupported application formats, overwriting, or retention limits prevented retrieval. This distinction is essential in an honest expert report."]}],"practice":{"title":"Recover the missing attachment","scenario":"A user deleted an invoice attachment, but the message remains in a webmail account.","steps":["Preserve the message and its headers through a supported export.","Check attachments, trash, backups, and provider-supported versions lawfully.","If examining an image, record carving settings and source offsets.","Compare recovered content with the original message and state what cannot be recovered."],"debrief":"A service export and a filesystem recovery answer different questions. Use the most reliable available source rather than assuming deleted means gone."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bsa"],"labId":"hash","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain logical, filesystem, and physical access and apply it to an investigation.","Explain deleted data and file carving and apply it to an investigation.","Explain recovery is a process, not a promise and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.7","prompt":"What is a common limitation of header-and-footer file carving?","options":["It always restores the original filename and owner.","It cannot process digital data.","It may struggle with fragmented files and lose filesystem context.","It makes all recovered content authentic."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Carving can recover content without the original directory entry, ownership information, or reliable timestamps.","unit":2}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
